WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
It's Friday night.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.800
You're in a four-player competition bowling game, the finest bowling game made.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.660
Championship bowling.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:33.280
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.840
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:01:04.120
I'm Joe. When you play these retro games in the timeline that we're playing them in, where modern games also exist and they don't know that they're retro, so we're just kind of retroactively reviewing them, but with no nostalgia, it's hard not to compare any bowling game, and specifically championship bowling, since it's the game we're reviewing this week, with Wii Bowling, which is part of Wii Sports, the perfect bowling game inside the perfect sports game.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:17.340
Championship bowling has a lot of work to do in this episode to convince me personally that it's essential, because sometimes when I think about bowling, I think about Wii Sports bowling before I even think about real-life bowling.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.000
Wow. I don't think about that at all.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:30.460
When I think of bowling, aside from real bowling, I think of that one arcade cabinet with the controller that's just a ball that you roll.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.020
That's bowling.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:45.580
See, I was also thinking a lot about Wii Bowling while playing this game, but not the motion controls, just the other controls that could be incorporated into a game that doesn't have motion controls.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.460
So yeah, I guess I wasn't even thinking about it, but I was comparing this game to Wii Bowling.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.980
But gotta love that ball control.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:02:03.680
So it's a bowling game, and you know, yeah, say no more, but is, we don't usually do this, but I'm just gonna ask right off the bat, is bowling enough to suck you in?

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.980
Like, is this enough for the game, like, just the idea that it's going to be bowling?

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.420
Does it stand a chance for you here?

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.380
I mean, no.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.340
When I saw this on the thing, I was like, okay, great.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.120
This stands no chance to make it on the essential games list.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:34.960
It might be fun, might be interesting, it might be a good simulation of bowling, but unless it's like an open world bowling,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:42.000
bowling, which is the only kind of game that exists in the future, then no.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.480
I'm personally going to keep my hand close to the vest here on whether or not I would think this could be essential,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.900
because I don't want to spoil my vote for the essential games list, so...

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.500
I can't wait until it's just no.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.300
It's not about spoiling.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.940
It's not, yeah, it's not about spoiling.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.160
I don't think... Sean did play it as if, you know,

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.100
the way he was talking just now was as if he's playing this game for the first time while we're recording,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.060
so technically he kept everything in the correct tense.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.160
Okay, that's fair.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.160
That's fair. I don't want, you know, I just want to make sure the listeners all know that, like,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.600
throughout this whole episode, no matter what I say, there's a chance that I would put it on the essential games list.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:25.720
Right, but Joe, the question remains, is bowling enough to suck you in?

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.640
I, alright, I'll say this.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.880
I think that when you say there's a bowling game, I'm already like,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.180
that's an uphill battle.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.800
But, it's possible.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.240
I can see a way.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.840
I can see it being possible.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.860
Like, if it's just a crazy, fun, like, two-player bowling game with, like, really good...

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.660
that, like, emulates it very well, maybe.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.800
But, let's say, like, okay, if you told me right off the bat that championship bowling,

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:01.180
like, you were, like, you actually got to, like, walk around the city in which the bowling alley was,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.160
and then you'd get calls throughout the day from, like, a cousin of yours,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.940
and they'd ask, like, hey, would you like to go bowling with me?

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.580
And then you'd go, and you'd pick him up, and you'd go to the bowling alley,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.860
and then you'd bowl, like, ten frames.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.120
It's basically Grand Theft Auto, but there's a bowling minigame in it.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.320
Oh, yeah. Yeah, it's that.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.500
Well, actually, yeah, Joe, I don't know if you caught on,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.180
but you're describing Grand Theft Auto 4,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.560
in which there is a bowling minigame in that, in which your cousin calls you.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.400
That is exactly what I was doing, yes.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.560
I actually didn't even know that, yeah.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.640
That's so funny. I've never even played Grand Theft Auto 4.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.600
I was just like, it sounds like Grand Theft Auto.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.540
Alright, but to Sean's point, right, like, bowling in video games nowadays,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:50.820
is more like something that's included as a side minigame that you can find.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.740
You know, like how in Yakuza, like, there's all sorts of minigames in the casino,

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.560
or karaoke, or stuff like that.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.720
Like, that's what bowling is now.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.400
Nobody is releasing standalone bowling games anymore, to the best of my knowledge.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.440
Maybe on mobile phones they are, but definitely, like,

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.700
I can't think of a bowling game being announced for PS5.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.200
I just don't see it happening.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:18.420
That would be something else. Can't even get them to make tennis games anymore, and now we want bowling?

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.220
Yeah, I hear you.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.720
And it's, uh, I guess I'm just trying to play the crazy devil's advocate here,

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.700
because I'm, like, thinking of, like, when we played NES Golf.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.700
Not that we voted that essential, but I was like, that surprised me.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.300
Yeah. And I hear a bowling game, I'm like, okay, well, any sports game is going to have a very hard time with me,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.100
but maybe it'll surprise me.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.460
And look, we can make this game sound good.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.420
You can choose your player.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.380
You get to choose your ball.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.180
You get to choose your lane.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.980
Like, there are customizable options.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.520
The problem is, is that when you choose your player,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:59.420
it actually doesn't, like, affect their skill or their types of moves they do.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.120
It's just their handedness is all that changes.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.940
And choosing your ball is about choosing the weight of the ball.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:07.460
How do we get to pick the color?

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.640
Yeah, seven-pound ball is always the same color.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.100
That's how they know it's a seven-pound ball, Sean.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.580
Well, yeah, but, I mean, if you are a real professional,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.060
you can get whatever color you want it.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.600
And choosing your lane is an aesthetic choice.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.460
It doesn't create any crazy lanes where it has zigzags or something,

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:28.920
and you have to try to get around it.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.080
Yeah, that's how I want, like, mini-golf bowling.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.880
I will say, just like, as an aside,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:39.460
that this did pop up on our recording schedule at probably the perfect time,

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.340
because while I admittedly haven't gone bowling in years,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:48.100
I did just watch, like, a 30-minute video about, like, the science of bowling

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:55.540
and oil on lanes and asymmetrical weights in the middle of a bowling ball.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.020
So, like, I'm at peak bowling curiosity at this moment.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:05.560
Sean, at any moment, did they talk about control and power meters during that video?

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.280
In a way, but it was more so, like, you know, not that.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:17.500
Hmm. How close did this simulate, like, the oil patterns on the lane

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.660
or the physics of bowling to you?

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:25.940
I mean, the only thing that I think, to answer your question seriously,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.460
I think that there might be some simulation of an oil pattern

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:37.060
just in, like, when a ball hooks.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.580
Maybe that's something that they've got cooked up in there.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.580
But other than that, I can't really tell.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:44.960
Is the oil pattern the thing that makes the ball go straight

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.560
all the way down to one inch before the pins

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.340
and then hook a very sharp left turn right into the gutter?

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.980
That is exactly what that is, yes.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.280
Okay. I think for the sakes of this episode,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.140
we're just going to assume everybody knows the rules of bowling

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.540
because this is exactly that.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.960
This is 10-frame bowling.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:06.860
There are no other modes.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:12.180
There's no wacky new additions to video gamify it, which is fine.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.180
But I'm just saying this is the bowling that we all know and play.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.000
So with that in mind,

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.680
the thing that makes you able to be a bowler in this game

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:24.820
is that once you've chosen your character and the weight of your ball,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.420
you get to select where you want to stand within the lane

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.160
and then how much power you want to use to throw the ball

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:36.680
and how much spin you want to put on the ball.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.380
Power is done through a power bar,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.540
similar to what we've seen in most sports games

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.300
where you have to have some kind of indicator

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.780
of how hard you're throwing or how hard you want to hit a ball.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.740
Here, it's the same thing. It's just a swinging power meter that moves relatively fast.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.280
Control, on the other hand,

00:08:53.280 --> 00:09:00.860
is showing a ball moving on like a semicircle from left to right.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.860
And if you land the ball on the left side,

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.560
it spins to the left.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:07.900
And if you land the ball in the middle,

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.240
it goes straight forward.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.780
And if you spin the ball to the right,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.560
the bowling ball will also spin to the right.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:18.520
Control seems to be the element here

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.500
that is the least communicated of the three things.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.200
When you're standing in the lane,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:27.180
wherever you stand is where you're going to release the ball from.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:28.240
And that much is clear.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:31.180
Power, again, we've seen it in many games.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.500
So we're aware that like at the highest,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:34.740
you're going to throw it really hard.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:38.120
And if you screw up and happen to hit it less than halfway,

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:40.060
you're going to throw a pretty weak ball.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.060
But control, to me, seems like

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.240
if I'm throwing to the left or to the right extremes,

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:49.580
my ball should be curving pretty damn hard.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.540
And yet, it's almost encouraged

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.880
to go to the extremes here in the left and right

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.040
because of the nice spin that it puts on your ball

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.860
and the way that that hits the pins

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:04.080
better than throwing dead on, if you will.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.040
I don't know if you guys experienced that.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.720
I just thought control was the outlier here

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:11.880
in terms of expressing what I thought it meant.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.860
Yeah, so I felt like during this game

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:21.500
that it was actually missing an entire variable

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.140
that you're supposed to be inputting for.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:28.940
I would think that you should also be changing

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:35.800
the immediate angle that you're throwing the ball at.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:38.820
The only thing that you're controlling

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.800
is the spin that you're putting on the ball

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:44.100
and not so much are you going to throw it

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.020
a few degrees to the right or the left.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.660
You're always throwing the ball straight down

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:53.000
and then the only after effect

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.760
is that spin that finally catches

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.460
the grain of the wood at the end.

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.680
And yeah, it is sort of like you only...

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:04.720
I would only do the extremes

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.000
or maybe just above the extreme

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.300
because there really isn't any effect

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.620
for most of the shot.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.000
But I also don't really...

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.000
It took me a few tries just to understand

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.480
what control actually meant

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.360
because I don't know what that icon

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.520
is supposed to represent.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:26.220
Is that a bowling ball in a baby carriage?

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.180
I couldn't quite tell.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.500
Is that supposed to be a metal...

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:36.160
a thing that covers food

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.100
when it's being served at a fancy restaurant?

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:40.680
I'm seeing a guy with a jump rope

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.500
and he's about to make his...

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.440
He's at the top of the apex of the rope

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:46.740
and he's about to spin it around again.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:47.900
Oh my God, I see that, yeah.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:49.500
And there's just like blue between the rope and the rope.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.240
Wow, but from a very low perspective.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.920
Yeah, he's like laying on the ground almost.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:00.660
So yeah, there was just a little bit of uncertainty

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.380
when it came to what I was actually controlling here.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.820
Yeah, the exact same thought immediately

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:07.800
when I played this game is like,

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:13.160
I'm missing a major element of control, ironically,

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.580
where I cannot angle the direction I'm shooting.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:21.440
Like, I get that, that like, yeah, you're using this thing to curve it

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.740
and you can't just be like, you can't just input like a value

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:25.980
of how much you want to curve it

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.180
because it's not actually that easy in bowling.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.080
But it's also not actually this hard.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.260
You're never going to try and curve it to the left

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:33.920
and accidentally curve it to the right

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.880
because you did something a little too late.

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:37.420
Like in real bowling.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:39.100
So it's weird that this thing's going so fast.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.140
Same thing with the power.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.160
Like your power in bowling,

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.140
one of the things that you, you know,

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:47.420
you have pretty one-to-one control over.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:49.140
You're never going to like, oh, I accidentally,

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:51.440
maybe you will give it too much power, not enough power.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:54.580
But like, this is the fastest moving power bar

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:55.520
I think we've seen.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.760
I mean, right? So it's like, you might think you're going to get max power,

00:12:58.900 --> 00:12:59.760
but if you're a second off,

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.560
you're going to get absolutely no power at all.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.100
And it just feels like,

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.000
and I know we've had this kind of things

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:05.880
in other games before,

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:09.840
but it feels like it's giving you

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.360
the wrong type of challenge

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.520
to try to even remotely like bowling.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.760
Like the challenge should come with like

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:21.280
thinking of the angles and thinking of the aims.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.240
Yes. And since you can't turn the angle,

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:25.540
like I'm thinking of,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:26.820
I mean, I know it's a more advanced system,

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:28.440
but I'm thinking of Kirby's Dream Course.

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:29.860
Have you ever played that game?

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:32.200
Like that game is not super easy,

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:34.100
but you are selecting exactly the angles

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:37.300
you want to shoot at. And you have to think about like the geometry of it.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:39.740
Now that changes this game a lot,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.360
but yeah, go ahead.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.060
Even most bowling games,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.700
what they do is,

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:48.480
is after you've selected where you want to stand,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.420
they just kind of put like an arrow

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.500
where you were, you know,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:53.620
a dotted line or whatever,

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.980
and you just can then choose

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.260
how you want to curve it.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:00.460
Like that is something that could easily be done

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:01.560
on this NES game.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:03.300
There's nothing advanced about that

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.300
or like, you know, there's just no graphical way to do it.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.380
The dotted line thing

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.560
has just always been the way to show

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:11.720
which way you want to direct the ball.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.020
So I agree with both of you

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:14.840
that it's kind of confusing

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:15.820
that it's not there.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:18.100
To go back to the power thing,

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:20.240
like the reason why it's bad

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.380
is because anything less than like

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.720
three-fourths of the bar being filled

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.940
is basically like a waste of a shot almost.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.880
Like you might get like a couple pins or whatever,

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.500
or you might gutter out or something

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:33.740
because you threw it so weak.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:36.340
So you do need mostly max power

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:37.520
